cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  cdr_FS: concentration/dose-response model fitting for feature
  selection in high-content screening
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite both the software and the
  article listed under preferred-citation, in which the method was
  first published.
type: software
abstract: >-
  cdr_FS selects features in high-dimensional screening data by
  asking, of each feature, whether its distributional distance from
  the control grows with exposure in a way a concentration/dose-response
  curve describes better than a flat line does. The distance is the
  earth mover's (Wasserstein) distance between the control and the
  exposed populations. Six standard concentration-response models
  (Brain-Cousens BC4 and BC5, four-parameter log-logistic,
  four-parameter Weibull, linear and constant) are ranked by AIC and
  BIC, and a feature is retained when its linear slope is positive and
  the constant model is not the best fit. Survivors can then be
  collapsed by correlation and filtered for sparsity. The experimental
  design is declared in a configuration file rather than written into
  the code. Extracted from NIB-SI/HCS-proc, the pipeline published in
  Tome et al. 2026, and it reproduces that article's feature
  selection: both earth mover's distance tables, the two retained
  feature lists of 182 and 374 features, the 99 that survive
  correlation collapsing, and the 95 final features.
